  "summary": {
    "optimal_team_size": "3-8 agents per team. Per-agent overhead minimizes at 3.5-4.5ms in this range while keeping total coordination overhead under 34ms. Beyond 20 agents, total overhead exceeds 75ms with diminishing returns.",
    "optimal_connection_pattern": "Hybrid: hub for coordination/status (1.9x faster single-op median at 0.26ms), channels for inter-agent messaging under concurrency (1.9x better p95 at 55ms vs 105ms).",
    "optimal_ordering": "5-10 pipeline stages optimal. Per-stage overhead is ~0.88-1.07ms. Linear scaling confirmed; keep under 10 stages for sub-10ms total pipeline overhead.",
    "optimal_agent_ratio": "2-4 concurrent writers per SQLite database. Throughput drops from 3611 ops/s (1 thread) to 578 ops/s (8 threads). The cliff is at 4 threads where efficiency drops below 36%. Tail latency (p99) explodes from ~0ms to 34ms at 4 threads and 230ms at 32 threads.",
    "key_findings": [
      "All 45 tool regression tests pass (12 tools tested). No regressions detected.",
      "Coordination overhead is dominated by status updates (60-70% of total), not registration or reads.",
      "SQLite WAL handles up to 4 concurrent writers efficiently; beyond that, write serialization causes severe tail latency (p99 >50ms).",
      "Work stealing is double-steal safe under thundering herd conditions (10 threads, 50 items), but adds 120x tail latency vs single-consumer.",
      "Help protocol race resolution is correct: exactly 1 winner per race, no double-assignments across 5 concurrent races.",
      "Channels peer-to-peer messaging outperforms hub under concurrent load (p95: 55ms vs 105ms), but hub is faster for single-threaded coordination ops.",
      "smart_read achieves 91% average token savings vs full file reads, at ~31ms per operation.",
      "query_kb averages 41ms per operation with peak memory of 14.8MB - lightweight enough for frequent agent use.",
      "Pipeline overhead scales linearly at ~1ms/stage. No nonlinear bottlenecks detected up to 20 stages.",
      "Idle detection is extremely fast (0.04ms median) - suitable for high-frequency polling in coordinator loops."
    ]
